===Etymology 1===
{{etymon|en|id=event|:inh|enm:cas}}
From {{inh|en|enm|cas}}, from {{der|en|fro|cas|t=an event}}, from {{der|en|la|cāsus|t=a falling, a fall; accident, event, occurrence; occasion, opportunity; noun case}}, perfect passive participle of {{m|la|cadō|t=to fall, to drop}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# An actual [[event]], [[situation]], or [[fact]].
#: {{ux|en|For a change, in this '''case''', he was telling the truth.}}
#: {{ux|en|It is not the '''case''' that every unfamiliar phrase is an idiom.}}
#: {{ux|en|In '''case''' of fire, break glass. [sign on fire extinguisher holder in public space]}}
#: {{ux|en|Conditioning would require exposition to any sentence before being uttered, which isn’t the '''case'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Economist|date=2013-07-20|volume=408|issue=8845
|title=The attack of the MOOCs
|url=http://www.economist.com/news/http://www.economist.com/news/business/21582001-army-new-online-courses-scaring-wits-out-traditional-universities-can-they
|passage=Since the launch early last year of&nbsp;[&hellip;] two Silicon Valley start-ups offering free education through MOOCs, massive open online courses, the ivory towers of academia have been shaken to their foundations. University brands built in some '''cases''' over centuries have been forced to contemplate the possibility that information technology will rapidly make their existing business model obsolete.}}
# {{lb|en|now|rare}} A given [[condition]] or [[state]].
#* {{RQ:Warner Albions England|chapter=XXXVI|page=174|passage=Thus vvhilſt he hopt he hild her leaſt, ſo altereth the '''cace''' / VVith ſuch as ſhe, Ah ſuch it is to build on ſuch a face.}}
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|III|10|text=Ne wist he how to turne, nor to what place: / Was never wretched man in such a wofull '''cace'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1726|author=Nathan Bailey; John Worlidge|title=Dictionarium Rusticum, Urbanicum & Botanicum|passage=Mares which are over-fat, hold with much difficulty; whereas those that are but in good '''case''' and plump, conceive with the greatest readiness and ease.}}
# A specific [[matter]] or piece of [[work]], specifically defined within a [[profession]], usually in respect of a specific person and/or event; the set of tasks involved in addressing one such matter.
#: {{ux|en|It was one of the detective's easiest '''cases'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|Social workers should work on a maximum of forty active '''cases'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|The doctor told us of an interesting '''case''' he had treated that morning.}}
#* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=2|passage=We drove back to the office with some concern on my part at the prospect of so large a '''case'''. Sunning himself on the board steps, I saw for the first time Mr. Farquhar Fenelon Cooke.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1927|author=[http://openlibrary.org/authors/OL2416183A F. E. Penny]|chapter=4|title=Pulling the Strings|url=http://openlibrary.org/works/OL16814587W|passage=The '''case''' was that of a murder. It had an element of mystery about it, however, which was puzzling the authorities. A turban and loincloth soaked in blood had been found; also a staff. These properties were known to have belonged to a toddy drawer. He had disappeared.}}
# {{lb|en|academia}} An instance or event as a topic of study.
#: {{ux|en|The teaching consists of theory lessons and '''case''' studies.}}
#* {{quote-journal|1=en|year=2012|month=March-April|author=John T. Jost|volume=100|issue=2|page=162|magazine=w:American Scientist|title=Social Justice: Is It in Our Nature (and Our Future)?|url=http://www.americanscientist.org/bookshelf/pub/social-justice-is-it-in-our-nature-and-our-future|passage=He draws eclectically on studies of baboons, descriptive anthropological accounts of hunter-gatherer societies and, in a few '''cases''', the fossil record.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20120213062813/http://www.americanscientist.org/bookshelf/pub/social-justice-is-it-in-our-nature-and-our-future|archivedate=13 February 2012}}
# {{senseid|en|law}}{{lb|en|legal}} A legal proceeding; a [[lawsuit]] or [[prosecution]].
#* {{RQ:Orczy Miss Elliott|chapter=The Tremarn Case|passage=“Two or three months more went by ; the public were eagerly awaiting the arrival of this semi-exotic claimant to an English peerage, and sensations, surpassing those of the Tichbourne '''case''', were looked forward to with palpitating interest. {{...}}”}}
#* {{RQ:Time|title=Ukraine to Claim $44bn in Climate Damages From Russia. Why War Is So Bad For Emissions|date=Nov 20 2025|author=Simmone Shah|text=As the conflict has dragged on, prosecutors in Ukraine are now pursuing 247 '''cases''' of environmental war crimes against Russia in Ukrainian courts and the International Criminal Court, according to reporting published in April by the ''New York Times''.|url=https://time.com/7335449/ukraine-russia-war-climate-impact/|accessdate=16 December 2025|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20251208034642/https://time.com/7335449/ukraine-russia-war-climate-impact/|archivedate=8 December 2025}}
# {{senseid|en|Q128234}}{{lb|en|grammar}} A specific [[inflection]] of a [[word]] (particularly a noun, pronoun, or adjective) depending on its [[function]] in the [[sentence]].
#: {{ux|en|The accusative '''case''' most commonly indicates a direct object.}}
#: {{ux|en|Latin has six '''cases''', and remnants of a seventh.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1988|author=Andrew Radford|title=Transformational grammar: a first course|location=Cambridge, UK|publisher=Cambridge University Press|page=292|chapter=6
|passage=Now, the Subject of either an indicative or a subjunctive Clause is always assigned ''Nominative'' '''case''', as we see from:<br>(16) (a) &emsp; I know [that ''they''/*''them''/*''their'' leave for Hawaii tomorrow]<br><span style="color:var(--wikt-palette-white,white);">(16)</span> (b) &emsp; I demand [that ''they''/*''them''/*''their'' leave for Hawaii tomorrow]<br>By contrast, the Subject of an infinitive Clause is assigned ''Objective'' '''case''', as we see from:<br>(17) &emsp; I want [''them''/*''they''/*''their'' to leave for Hawaii tomorrow]<br>And the Subject of a ''gerund'' Clause is assigned either ''Objective'' or ''Genitive'' '''case''': cf.<br> (18) &emsp; I don't like the idea of [''them''/''their''/*''they'' leaving for Hawaii tomorrow]}}
# {{lb|en|grammar|uncountable}} [[grammatical case|Grammatical cases]] and their meanings taken either as a topic in general or within a specific [[language]].
#: {{ux|en|Jane has been studying '''case''' in Caucasian languages.}}
#: {{ux|en|Latin is a language that employs '''case'''.}}
# {{senseid|en|instance of a specific condition or set of symptoms}} {{lb|en|medicine}} An instance of a specific [[condition]] or set of [[symptom]]s.
#: {{ant|en|noncase}}
#: {{hypo|en|index case}}
#: {{cot|en|patient#Noun}}
#: {{ux|en|There were another five '''cases''' reported overnight.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2013|author=Gillian Russell; Delia Graff Fara|title=Routledge Companion to Philosophy of Language|page=144
|passage=We turn next to the puzzle of borderlineness: If Harry is intermediate between clear '''cases''' and clear noncases of baldness, “Is Harry bald?” seems to have no good, direct, answer.}}
# {{lb|en|programming}} A section of [[code]] representing one of the [[action]]s of a [[conditional]] [[switch]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2004|author=Rick Miller|title=C++ for Artists|passage=Place a break statement at the end of every '''case''' to prevent case fall-through.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2011|author=Stephen Prata|title=C++ Primer Plus|page=275|passage=Execution does not automatically stop at the next '''case'''.}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} A [[love affair]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1867|title=The Young Ladies' Journal|page=467
|passage=Poor fellow, just as I thought! It's a '''case''' with him, anybody can see that. He is thinking about Christine, for a certainty. Lovers always take to stargazing and moonlight dreaming — it's part of their complaint.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1876|title=The New York Drama|volume=1-2|page=1
|passage=I thought it only an amourette when you told me. It was a fire — a conflagration; subdue it. I saw it was a '''case''', and I advised you to try — dissipation.}}

=====Usage notes=====
* In medicine, in precise and respectful usage, a case is not a patient and a patient is not a case, whereas a patient has a case. In [[Appendix:Glossary#loosely|loose]] usage, however, the words are often treated synonymously.

